  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 409 PM EDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nOpted to extend the flash flood watch until 2 am EDT for areas \nfurther south and east given expected rainfall through late tonight-\nwhich is where most of the rain has fallen over the past week and \nimpacts linger. Additional totals of 1-2\" are possible through \nMonday morning as a shortwave or two move through, which could \nexacerbate any flooding in these areas. Much of the high res \ndevelops heavier rainfall within some more convective elements this \nevening, however as of this writing we are pretty meager in terms of \nheavy rain. Expect a period of isentropic ascent to continue through \nat least the evening as a low pressure system develops along the \nstationary front with this first shortwave. Still have decent warm \ncloud depth, pwats around 2\" and a LLJ of around 25-30 knots, so \nheavy rain is still a possibility. Thunderstorms are also possible. \n\nOtherwise, expect a drying trend late Monday into the rest of the \nweek, with mainly intermittent low chances (20-30%) as we sit on the \nperiphery of upper level ridge/upper low, with surface high pressure \nbelow (particularly Thursday into the weekend). It will be more fall \nlike, with highs in the 60s, low 70s and then lows falling into the \nupper 40s, 50s. By the weekend, we warm up into the low-mid 70s \n(warmest Sun).",
